1
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:13,860
In 2024, a team of geneticists did
something no one had done before.

2
00:00:14,388 --> 00:00:18,680
They pulled DNA from the bones
hidden inside the famous plaster

3
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,667
casts of Pompeii.

4
00:00:21,017 --> 00:00:25,240
For a hundred and fifty years,
those casts had told us stories.

5
00:00:25,856 --> 00:00:27,900
A mother, holding her child.

6
00:00:28,436 --> 00:00:31,020
Two sisters, in one last embrace.

7
00:00:31,688 --> 00:00:34,560
The DNA said those stories were wrong.

8
00:00:35,138 --> 00:00:37,660
The adult holding the child was a man,

9
00:00:37,700 --> 00:00:40,880
and he was not related
to the child at all.

10
00:00:41,358 --> 00:00:44,840
So what really happened on
the last day of Pompeii?

11
00:00:45,279 --> 00:00:47,580
Not the legend, but the evidence.

12
00:00:52,734 --> 00:00:54,960
This is Ancient World Files.

13
00:01:00,592 --> 00:01:06,040
In the first century A.D., Pompeii was
a busy Roman town on the Bay of Naples,

14
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:07,780
in southern Italy.

15
00:01:08,297 --> 00:01:11,100
About eleven thousand people lived there.

16
00:01:11,464 --> 00:01:14,500
It had a forum, temples, two theaters,

17
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:18,400
an amphitheater, public baths,
and dozens of bakeries,

18
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:19,640
bars, and workshops.

19
00:01:20,279 --> 00:01:22,180
Its walls were covered with writing:

20
00:01:22,640 --> 00:01:25,580
election posters, advertisements, jokes,

21
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:27,400
insults, and love notes.

22
00:01:28,007 --> 00:01:31,600
It was an ordinary place,
full of ordinary people.

23
00:01:32,153 --> 00:01:36,440
One visitor scratched a complaint
that survives in several places

24
00:01:36,480 --> 00:01:37,920
around the city.

25
00:01:38,474 --> 00:01:42,520
It reads: I am amazed, O wall,
that you have not collapsed,

26
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:45,860
carrying the tired scrawls
of so many writers.

27
00:01:46,510 --> 00:01:52,020
In one bakery, archaeologists found eighty
one loaves of bread still inside the oven,

28
00:01:52,060 --> 00:01:53,600
turned to carbon.

29
00:01:54,172 --> 00:01:58,320
And above the town rose a
mountain covered in vineyards.

30
00:01:58,801 --> 00:02:01,020
The Romans called it Vesuvius.

31
00:02:01,588 --> 00:02:04,460
Almost no one thought of it as dangerous.

32
00:02:05,093 --> 00:02:09,840
The Greek geographer Strabo had noticed
that the rocks near its summit looked

33
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:13,540
scorched, as if they had
once been eaten by fire.

34
00:02:14,052 --> 00:02:16,960
But the slopes were green,
the soil was rich,

35
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:19,040
and the local wine was famous.

36
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:24,280
There had been a warning.

37
00:02:24,867 --> 00:02:29,180
Seventeen years earlier,
a powerful earthquake struck the region.

38
00:02:29,762 --> 00:02:32,280
Temples fell and houses cracked.

39
00:02:32,905 --> 00:02:38,678
The philosopher Seneca wrote that a flock
of six hundred sheep died near Pompeii.

40
00:02:39,028 --> 00:02:42,800
When the end came, parts of the
city were still being rebuilt.

41
00:02:43,371 --> 00:02:46,940
In one house, painters had
left a wall half finished.

42
00:02:47,499 --> 00:02:49,420
And in the days before the eruption,

43
00:02:49,940 --> 00:02:52,120
small tremors shook the area again.

44
00:02:52,609 --> 00:02:55,020
But tremors were common in Campania,

45
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:57,000
and no one was alarmed.

46
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:08,860
We know what happened next
because of one eyewitness.

47
00:03:09,635 --> 00:03:13,880
His name was Pliny the Younger,
and he was seventeen years old.

48
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:16,860
He was staying with his uncle at Misenum,

49
00:03:17,080 --> 00:03:21,880
at the northern tip of the bay,
about thirty kilometers from the mountain.

50
00:03:22,466 --> 00:03:27,580
His uncle was Pliny the Elder, a famous
scholar who had written an enormous

51
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:30,560
encyclopedia of the natural world.

52
00:03:31,033 --> 00:03:35,320
He was also the commander of
the Roman fleet stationed there.

53
00:03:35,674 --> 00:03:37,800
Around one o'clock in the afternoon,

54
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:42,600
Pliny's mother pointed out a
cloud of unusual size and shape.

55
00:03:43,095 --> 00:03:47,820
His uncle called for his shoes and
climbed to a spot with a better view.

56
00:03:48,332 --> 00:03:51,180
Pliny later compared
it to an umbrella pine,

57
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:55,780
a tall trunk rising high and
then spreading out into branches.

58
00:03:56,489 --> 00:04:01,620
Today, eruptions like this one are
called Plinian eruptions, after him.

59
00:04:02,090 --> 00:04:08,080
Volcanologists estimate the column rose
more than thirty kilometers into the sky.

60
00:04:08,663 --> 00:04:12,380
Vesuvius has erupted
many times since then.

61
00:04:12,846 --> 00:04:15,220
This film shows its most recent eruption,

62
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:21,300
in 1944, and that column was far
smaller than the one Pliny saw.

63
00:04:21,875 --> 00:04:25,700
Inside the column of 79
was a mix of hot gas,

64
00:04:26,180 --> 00:04:27,340
ash, and pumice.

65
00:04:27,819 --> 00:04:33,460
Pumice is volcanic rock so full of gas
bubbles that it can float on water.

66
00:04:34,013 --> 00:04:36,780
The wind was blowing toward the southeast.

67
00:04:37,444 --> 00:04:42,500
That put Pompeii, about ten kilometers
from the vent, directly underneath.

68
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:46,740
Within an hour or so,
the sky over Pompeii went dark,

69
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:49,542
and stones began to fall from it.

70
00:04:57,892 --> 00:05:00,220
The pumice came down like hail.

71
00:05:00,872 --> 00:05:04,240
It was not hot enough to burn,
and each stone was light,

72
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:05,820
but they did not stop.

73
00:05:06,372 --> 00:05:11,100
For about eighteen hours,
pumice and ash rained down on Pompeii,

74
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:14,440
piling up at about fifteen
centimeters an hour.

75
00:05:14,891 --> 00:05:15,920
Streets filled.

76
00:05:16,650 --> 00:05:18,180
Doorways were blocked.

77
00:05:18,775 --> 00:05:22,540
By the end, the layer was
nearly three meters deep.

78
00:05:23,044 --> 00:05:25,660
Many people fled,
carrying what they could:

79
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:30,320
coins, jewelry, keys,
small statues of their household gods.

80
00:05:30,982 --> 00:05:34,340
Others sheltered indoors,
hoping to wait it out.

81
00:05:34,835 --> 00:05:37,380
But the weight of the
pumice on the roofs grew,

82
00:05:37,640 --> 00:05:39,900
and the roofs began to give way.

83
00:05:40,407 --> 00:05:44,800
Of the victims found at Pompeii,
more than a third lay in the pumice layer,

84
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:48,220
many of them crushed by
falling roofs and walls.

85
00:05:48,841 --> 00:05:52,080
Across the bay, Pliny the
Elder made a decision.

86
00:05:52,674 --> 00:05:55,760
A message arrived from
a woman named Rectina,

87
00:05:56,020 --> 00:05:58,360
whose villa lay at the
foot of the mountain,

88
00:05:58,780 --> 00:06:00,220
begging to be rescued.

89
00:06:00,809 --> 00:06:03,700
He ordered the warships
of the fleet to launch.

90
00:06:04,213 --> 00:06:08,520
Others were fleeing the coast,
but he steered straight toward the danger,

91
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:13,140
dictating notes on the shape and
movement of the cloud as he went.

92
00:06:13,663 --> 00:06:16,020
When his helmsman urged him to turn back,

93
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:20,220
he is said to have replied:
Fortune favors the brave.

94
00:06:20,848 --> 00:06:25,220
What had begun as a scientific
trip became a rescue mission.

95
00:06:25,751 --> 00:06:27,680
His nephew stayed behind.

96
00:06:28,308 --> 00:06:30,800
He said he would rather keep studying.

97
00:06:31,354 --> 00:06:34,640
Near the shore, hot ash and
pumice fell on the decks,

98
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:37,300
and the coast was blocked by debris.

99
00:06:37,979 --> 00:06:41,040
So the admiral turned south, to Stabiae,

100
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:44,080
where his friend Pomponianus had a villa.

101
00:06:44,455 --> 00:06:47,320
To calm everyone around him, he bathed,

102
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:49,200
ate dinner, and went to sleep.

103
00:06:49,785 --> 00:06:54,140
During the night, the courtyard outside
his room filled with ash and pumice,

104
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:55,920
and the buildings shook.

105
00:06:59,483 --> 00:07:03,140
Then, sometime after midnight,
the eruption changed.

106
00:07:03,614 --> 00:07:07,260
The great column began to
collapse under its own weight.

107
00:07:07,795 --> 00:07:11,140
And when it fell,
it became something far deadlier.

108
00:07:11,645 --> 00:07:15,780
A pyroclastic flow is an
avalanche of superheated gas,

109
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:22,160
ash, and rock that races across the ground
at more than a hundred kilometers an hour.

110
00:07:22,673 --> 00:07:24,300
It cannot be outrun.

111
00:07:24,946 --> 00:07:27,140
The first one reached Herculaneum,

112
00:07:27,180 --> 00:07:29,840
on the coast west of the volcano.

113
00:07:30,505 --> 00:07:36,220
Flow after flow would bury the town under
about twenty meters of volcanic material,

114
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:38,880
far deeper than Pompeii.

115
00:07:39,318 --> 00:07:43,700
For centuries, archaeologists
believed that almost everyone in

116
00:07:43,740 --> 00:07:45,800
Herculaneum had escaped.

117
00:07:49,396 --> 00:07:54,280
Then, in the nineteen eighties,
excavators reached the ancient shoreline.

118
00:07:54,769 --> 00:07:57,320
In the stone boat sheds facing the sea,

119
00:07:57,540 --> 00:07:58,920
they found skeletons.

120
00:07:59,653 --> 00:08:01,140
Hundreds of them.

121
00:08:01,670 --> 00:08:05,340
More than three hundred people
had gone down to the water,

122
00:08:05,660 --> 00:08:08,140
perhaps hoping to be rescued by boat.

123
00:08:08,705 --> 00:08:11,160
Most of the men were found on the beach,

124
00:08:11,320 --> 00:08:14,240
while women and children
crowded into the vaults.

125
00:08:14,766 --> 00:08:17,480
The heat killed them almost instantly.

126
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:23,260
And in a building in the town, a young
man was found lying on a wooden bed.

127
00:08:23,781 --> 00:08:28,740
In 2020, researchers reported that
fragments of his brain had been

128
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:30,060
turned into glass.

129
00:08:38,616 --> 00:08:41,660
At dawn on the second day,
there was no dawn.

130
00:08:42,221 --> 00:08:46,020
At Misenum, the young Pliny
and his mother finally fled,

131
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:51,420
and he described the darkness as being
in a closed room with the lamp put out.

132
00:08:51,911 --> 00:08:55,660
He heard women shrieking,
children crying, men shouting.

133
00:08:56,324 --> 00:08:58,320
People were calling for their parents,

134
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:01,000
their children, their husbands and wives,

135
00:09:01,040 --> 00:09:04,140
trying to recognize each
other by their voices.

136
00:09:04,827 --> 00:09:07,060
Many believed there were no gods left,

137
00:09:07,100 --> 00:09:11,120
and that this was the last,
eternal night of the world.

138
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:14,420
His mother begged him to leave her behind.

139
00:09:15,017 --> 00:09:18,720
She was old and slow, she said,
and he could still escape.

140
00:09:19,394 --> 00:09:22,460
He refused, took her hand,
and made her walk.

141
00:09:23,051 --> 00:09:28,220
Ash fell on them so thickly that they had
to keep standing up and shaking it off,

142
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:31,520
or they would have been buried
and crushed by its weight.

143
00:09:35,060 --> 00:09:37,680
At Pompeii, around seven in the morning,

144
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
a pyroclastic surge
reached the city walls.

145
00:09:41,647 --> 00:09:43,360
It swept through the streets.

146
00:09:43,991 --> 00:09:47,020
No one who was still in the city survived.

147
00:09:47,591 --> 00:09:52,620
For a long time, it was thought that
the victims had suffocated in the ash.

148
00:09:53,099 --> 00:09:58,380
But a study published in 2010 concluded
that the main killer was heat.

149
00:09:58,936 --> 00:10:02,880
Even inside buildings,
ten kilometers from the volcano,

150
00:10:03,340 --> 00:10:07,580
temperatures of at least two hundred
and fifty degrees Celsius would have

151
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:09,540
killed almost instantly.

152
00:10:10,109 --> 00:10:14,400
Their bodies were caught in the
positions they were in at that moment.

153
00:10:14,970 --> 00:10:20,700
At Stabiae, the same morning, Pliny the
Elder and his companions tied pillows to

154
00:10:20,740 --> 00:10:26,120
their heads to protect themselves from
falling stones and went down to the shore.

155
00:10:26,680 --> 00:10:28,880
The sea was too rough to escape.

156
00:10:29,451 --> 00:10:32,440
Then came flames, and a smell of sulfur.

157
00:10:33,029 --> 00:10:35,780
The old admiral collapsed on the beach.

158
00:10:36,361 --> 00:10:38,560
His body was found two days later,

159
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:41,360
intact, looking, his nephew wrote,

160
00:10:41,540 --> 00:10:43,680
more like a man asleep than dead.

161
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:47,220
More flows followed, layer after layer.

162
00:10:47,727 --> 00:10:51,960
By the end of the second day,
Pompeii lay under four to six meters of

163
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:53,280
ash and pumice.

164
00:10:53,827 --> 00:10:56,400
When daylight finally returned to Misenum,

165
00:10:56,680 --> 00:11:00,080
the sun shone weakly, as if in an eclipse.

166
00:11:00,741 --> 00:11:04,100
Everything the young Pliny
could see was buried in ash,

167
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:06,100
as if under deep snow.

168
00:11:06,685 --> 00:11:11,300
Only the tops of a few walls
showed above a new, grey landscape.

169
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:15,500
And then, slowly, even the
city's name was forgotten.

170
00:11:23,983 --> 00:11:28,500
For more than sixteen centuries,
Pompeii lay under fields and vineyards.

171
00:11:28,949 --> 00:11:32,860
Digging at Herculaneum began in 1738,

172
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:35,220
on the orders of the King of Naples,

173
00:11:35,260 --> 00:11:37,960
and at Pompeii ten years later.

174
00:11:38,453 --> 00:11:43,160
At first, the excavators did not
even know which city they had found.

175
00:11:43,727 --> 00:11:48,480
It was only in 1763 that an
inscription naming the town of

176
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:50,060
Pompeii was found.

177
00:11:50,567 --> 00:11:52,940
The early excavations were treasure hunts:

178
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:57,940
statues, paintings, and jewelry were
carried off to the royal palace.

179
00:11:58,528 --> 00:12:03,140
That changed with an archaeologist
named Giuseppe Fiorelli.

180
00:12:03,675 --> 00:12:08,220
Fiorelli took charge of
the excavations in 1863.

181
00:12:09,037 --> 00:12:12,360
Workers had noticed strange
hollows in the hardened ash,

182
00:12:12,400 --> 00:12:14,849
with bones inside them.

183
00:12:15,199 --> 00:12:18,200
Fiorelli understood what they were.

184
00:12:18,561 --> 00:12:21,440
The ash had hardened around
the bodies of the victims,

185
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:25,980
and as the bodies decayed,
they left perfect empty molds.

186
00:12:26,584 --> 00:12:29,600
He had the hollows filled
with liquid plaster.

187
00:12:30,188 --> 00:12:33,340
When the plaster set and
the ash was chipped away,

188
00:12:33,640 --> 00:12:36,006
the people of Pompeii reappeared.

189
00:12:36,356 --> 00:12:37,800
Folds of clothing.

190
00:12:38,421 --> 00:12:39,600
Sandal straps.

191
00:12:40,213 --> 00:12:41,213
Hair.

192
00:12:41,536 --> 00:12:42,536
Faces.

193
00:12:43,018 --> 00:12:45,220
People lying where they fell.

194
00:12:45,794 --> 00:12:48,340
A dog twisting against its chain.

195
00:12:48,905 --> 00:12:51,840
More than a century later, in 2015,

196
00:12:52,200 --> 00:12:56,180
researchers began scanning
the casts with CT machines.

197
00:12:56,741 --> 00:13:01,740
Inside, they found skeletons,
and teeth in remarkably good condition,

198
00:13:02,020 --> 00:13:04,600
which they linked to a diet low in sugar.

199
00:13:05,149 --> 00:13:07,180
In a garden at the edge of the city,

200
00:13:07,440 --> 00:13:09,640
now called the Garden of the Fugitives,

201
00:13:10,160 --> 00:13:12,020
thirteen people were found together.

202
00:13:12,550 --> 00:13:16,180
The casts made Pompeii
famous around the world.

203
00:13:16,638 --> 00:13:19,540
But they also made it
easy to tell stories.

204
00:13:20,069 --> 00:13:22,660
Guides and scholars
gave the figures roles:

205
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:26,040
the mother, the lovers,
the slave, the sisters.

206
00:13:26,691 --> 00:13:28,860
Those stories were based on posture,

207
00:13:29,360 --> 00:13:30,700
size, and jewelry.

208
00:13:31,243 --> 00:13:33,185
No one could test them.

209
00:13:33,535 --> 00:13:34,720
Until now.

210
00:13:43,244 --> 00:13:46,280
The first new evidence was about the date.

211
00:13:46,853 --> 00:13:50,980
For centuries, the eruption was
dated to the twenty fourth of August,

212
00:13:51,020 --> 00:13:55,340
from the most common manuscript
version of Pliny's letter.

213
00:13:55,952 --> 00:13:59,060
But the letter survives
only in medieval copies,

214
00:13:59,380 --> 00:14:01,560
and the copies give different dates.

215
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:05,160
And the archaeology never
quite fit the summer.

216
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:07,960
Victims seemed to wear heavy clothing,

217
00:14:08,540 --> 00:14:13,580
braziers had been in use, and the shops
held autumn food like pomegranates.

218
00:14:14,098 --> 00:14:18,080
Wine jars had already been
sealed after the grape harvest.

219
00:14:18,684 --> 00:14:23,940
A coin found with one victim carried
a title of the emperor Titus that,

220
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:28,380
according to many historians,
he only received in September.

221
00:14:31,944 --> 00:14:37,360
Then, in 2018, archaeologists found
a line of charcoal writing on a wall

222
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:39,760
in a house being renovated.

223
00:14:40,409 --> 00:14:44,620
It gives a date: the sixteenth day
before the Kalends of November,

224
00:14:44,940 --> 00:14:47,040
which is the seventeenth of October.

225
00:14:47,663 --> 00:14:51,400
It gives no year,
but charcoal writing fades quickly,

226
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:54,900
so it was probably written
just days before the eruption.

227
00:14:55,501 --> 00:15:00,980
Many researchers now believe Vesuvius
erupted on or around the twenty

228
00:15:01,020 --> 00:15:02,180
fourth of October.

229
00:15:05,773 --> 00:15:09,980
The second new evidence came
from inside the casts themselves.

230
00:15:10,650 --> 00:15:13,560
Many of them still hold bones and teeth.

231
00:15:14,135 --> 00:15:18,180
In 2024, a team from the
University of Florence,

232
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:23,820
Harvard, and the Max Planck Institute
published DNA from fourteen of them.

233
00:15:24,325 --> 00:15:26,400
In the House of the Golden Bracelet,

234
00:15:26,720 --> 00:15:31,620
an adult wearing a gold bracelet
was found with a child on their lap.

235
00:15:32,121 --> 00:15:36,400
For generations, they were
described as a mother and her child.

236
00:15:36,951 --> 00:15:39,860
The DNA showed that the adult was a man,

237
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:42,680
and that he was not related to the child.

238
00:15:43,268 --> 00:15:45,740
Two other victims, locked in an embrace,

239
00:15:45,900 --> 00:15:50,020
had been imagined as sisters,
or as a mother and daughter.

240
00:15:50,552 --> 00:15:53,600
At least one of them was genetically male.

241
00:15:54,152 --> 00:15:59,380
The DNA also showed that the people
of Pompeii had diverse origins,

242
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:03,180
many with recent roots in
the eastern Mediterranean.

243
00:16:03,934 --> 00:16:07,760
The researchers did not say the
old stories were impossible.

244
00:16:08,351 --> 00:16:12,860
They said those stories had been shaped
by modern assumptions about family,

245
00:16:13,100 --> 00:16:16,760
about gender, and about who
would hold whom at the end.

246
00:16:20,199 --> 00:16:23,540
The third new evidence
came from Herculaneum,

247
00:16:23,820 --> 00:16:27,360
from a library of scrolls
burned into lumps of carbon.

248
00:16:27,926 --> 00:16:33,800
Without opening one of them, researchers
used CT scans and artificial intelligence

249
00:16:33,840 --> 00:16:37,080
to read fifteen columns of hidden text.

250
00:16:37,598 --> 00:16:41,400
The words had not been read
for almost two thousand years.

251
00:16:49,901 --> 00:16:53,520
So, what really happened
on the last day of Pompeii?

252
00:16:53,887 --> 00:16:59,318
Most people probably escaped: about eleven
hundred and fifty bodies have been found

253
00:16:59,358 --> 00:17:03,766
in Pompeii, out of a town
of around eleven thousand.

254
00:17:04,116 --> 00:17:07,740
Others may still lie in the
unexcavated parts of the city,

255
00:17:07,780 --> 00:17:10,340
or on the roads where they fell.

256
00:17:10,865 --> 00:17:14,320
Those who died were killed by
collapsing roofs in the night,

257
00:17:14,360 --> 00:17:17,380
or by the heat of the surge at dawn.

258
00:17:17,982 --> 00:17:21,440
They died, most likely,
not in August but in the autumn,

259
00:17:21,480 --> 00:17:24,380
among the new wine and the pomegranates.

260
00:17:24,971 --> 00:17:28,620
And the people we have looked at for
a century and a half were not quite

261
00:17:28,660 --> 00:17:29,820
who we thought.

262
00:17:30,387 --> 00:17:34,560
Not a mother, but a man holding
a child who was not his own.

263
00:17:35,089 --> 00:17:38,060
Not sisters, but strangers, or friends,

264
00:17:38,360 --> 00:17:40,260
or something we will never know.

265
00:17:44,240 --> 00:17:47,980
Pompeii is often called
a city frozen in time.

266
00:17:48,526 --> 00:17:51,360
But it is not frozen: every new tool,

267
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:56,920
from the scanner to the DNA sequencer
to the AI that reads burned scrolls,

268
00:17:57,220 --> 00:17:59,080
changes what it tells us.

269
00:17:59,440 --> 00:18:04,522
The city died in a single day, and we
are still learning what happened in it.

270
00:18:11,872 --> 00:18:14,680
Thank you for watching
Ancient World Files.

